one recent independent poll shows him up eleven points; that outweighs an older Republican-sponsored survey, though Tijerina's $1.4 million in fundraising and Trump's campaigning keep this well short of Safe.", "watch": "Fresh polling or a late wave of national Republican spending behind Tijerina could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Henry Cuellar (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Henry Cuellar, a longtime Democratic incumbent, seeks reelection in a South Texas district that leans Republican by about three points but has long favored his moderate personal brand. Tano Tijerina, the Webb County judge and a recent party switcher, gives Republicans a credible, well-funded challenger, and Donald Trump has appeared with him at national events. Still, most race raters call the contest Lean or Likely Democratic, and prediction markets put Cuellar's chances near 85 percent.\n\nMoney is not the deciding issue: Cuellar has raised about $2.2 million to Tijerina's $1.4 million. Polling is sparse and mixed — an August survey of likely voters showed Cuellar up eleven points, while a June Republican-sponsored poll had him down eight. A Green Party candidate may draw a few votes from the left. The forecast expects Cuellar to win by roughly seven points, with real uncertainty given the limited data.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9009, "margin": 7.69}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8765, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8779, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8804, "margin": 7.0}]}